---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import HourlyRateCalculator from '../../components/HourlyRateCalculator.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="Freelancer Hourly Rate Calculator" description="Work backward from your desired annual income to the hourly rate you actually need to charge.">
  <HourlyRateCalculator client:load slot="tool" />
  <h2>Why "just divide salary by 2000 hours" is wrong</h2>
  <p>Freelancers rarely bill 40 hours a week — admin, proposals, and downtime eat into billable capacity. This calculator accounts for realistic billable hours and business expenses, not just take-home income.</p>

  <h2>Common Hourly Rate Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Assuming 100% of work hours are billable</strong>, ignoring time spent on admin, marketing, and unpaid proposal work.</li>
    <li><strong>Forgetting to account for business expenses and taxes</strong> that an employee wouldn't have to budget for separately.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use This Hourly Rate Calculator</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Setting a freelance hourly rate that actually supports your target income</li>
    <li>Deciding whether a client's proposed rate is financially viable</li>
    <li>Understanding the real gap between billable rate and take-home pay</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Hourly Rate FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>What percentage of hours are typically billable?</strong> Many freelancers find 60-75% of working hours are actually billable once admin, proposals, and non-billable communication are accounted for — this varies significantly by how the work is structured.</p>
  <p><strong>Should I include vacation time in the calculation?</strong> Yes — if you want your annual income target to hold even with time off, reduce your assumed working weeks accordingly rather than assuming 52 full weeks of billable work.</p>
</ToolLayout>
